<%# THE POOL BEHIND THIS CATEGORY, IN THE WORDS OF WHAT THAT POOL ACTUALLY IS. Local: `presenter`
    (CategoryBudgetPresenter).

    THE DEFECT THIS CARD HELD (§7a's savings-chrome family, its last known member). The card is
    older than envelopes and rendered savings chrome for EVERY pool it was handed: a budget
    envelope read "Savings Pool" with a bare "Target:" and no figure after it, and — found while
    fixing that — an ACCOUNT read "Savings Pool / Target: $1,000.00 / -30% complete", a savings
    progress bar drawn on a buffer, in red, on this app's own demo data. None of the three
    sentences was true of either. Task 5 then landed the budget block directly ABOVE this card, so
    the page said "Envelope" and "Savings Pool" about one pool, four inches apart.

    THE TYPE IS ASKED OF THE POOL, not of the category. `Category#pool_covered?` is
    `expense? && pool_id.present?` and says yes to an account, which is exactly how the account
    arm of the block above went wrong before it was corrected; the pool's own enum cannot.

    ONE VOCABULARY, NOT A NEW ONE. The envelope arm says how the envelope is doing through
    `shared/_pool_status` — the same span, the same words and the same red as Home's pools band
    and the Budget page's group headers — so a user reading this page and /budget on one afternoon
    is told one thing. It passes the presenter as the row, so both label suffixes are threaded by
    construction and this new caller cannot repeat the omission the whole-plan review caught twice.

    The account arm reads a BALANCE and no state: an account is the buffer (§7.1), its target is a
    health marker rather than a goal, and `PoolStatus` would word it as saving toward one.

    THE HEADING IS `Pool#noun` (2d whole-plan review, fix 2), which is what finally closed the
    three-noun defect this card was the loudest member of. Its savings arm said "Savings Pool"
    while the budget block above said "Envelope" and the entry form's impact card said "goal" —
    about ONE pool, on one afternoon. The classifiers never disagreed; only the words did. %>
<% pool = presenter.pool %>
<div class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 p-6"
     data-pool-card
     data-pool-card-type="<%= pool.pool_type %>">
  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4"><%= pool.noun.capitalize %></h2>

  <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-4">
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= pool.name %></h3>
      <%# A TARGET ONLY WHERE ONE IS A GOAL. A budget envelope's `target_amount` is nil, which is
          how "Target:" came to print with nothing after it, and an account's is the buffer marker
          the user set — a health line, never a thing being saved toward (Home's account header
          says "target" for exactly this reason and says "buffer now" first). %>
      <% if pool.pool_type_savings? %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-1">Target: <%= number_to_currency(pool.target_amount) %></p>
      <% elsif pool.pool_type_account? %>
        <%# RED WHEN IT IS NEGATIVE, matching `home/_account`'s header: an overdraft is the loudest
            thing in the app, and a buffer at -$300.00 printed in the same grey as a healthy one
            is this page being quieter about it than every other screen. %>
        <p class="text-sm mt-1 <%= presenter.balance.negative? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-500" %>">
          buffer now <strong><%= number_to_currency(presenter.balance) %></strong>
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <div class="h-10 w-10 bg-brand rounded-full flex items-center justify-center">
      <% if pool.pool_type_savings? %>
        <%= heroicon "banknotes", options: { class: "h-5 w-5 text-white" } %>
      <% elsif pool.pool_type_account? %>
        <%= heroicon "building-library", options: { class: "h-5 w-5 text-white" } %>
      <% else %>
        <%= heroicon "wallet", options: { class: "h-5 w-5 text-white" } %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# THE ENVELOPE'S STANDING, in the app's one row vocabulary. Rendered from four spaces in
      because that is the column `shared/_pool_status` is written to emit at — see its header. %>
  <%# ITS OWN HOOK, NOT `data-envelope-status`. The budget block above this card carries that one,
      and both render on one page for a pool-covered expense category — a page-wide
      `find("[data-envelope-status]")` raises Capybara::Ambiguous the moment two elements answer to
      it, which would have turned an existing scoped assertion into a failure for a reason having
      nothing to do with what it asserts. %>
  <% if pool.pool_type_budget? %>
  <p class="mb-4" data-pool-card-status>
    <%= render "shared/pool_status", row: presenter %>
  </p>
  <% end %>

  <%# THE PROGRESS BAR IS THE SAVINGS ARM'S ALONE. `progress_percentage` measures a balance against
      a goal and clamps at 100 but not at zero, so on a pool with no goal — or an overdrawn one —
      it draws a bar the wrong way and labels it "-30% complete". A goal is the only place the
      question means anything. %>
  <% if pool.pool_type_savings? && pool.target_amount.present? && pool.target_amount.positive? %>
    <%# `progress_percentage` now floors at zero as well as capping at 100, so the `< 0` arms below
        are unreachable and kept only as belt to that brace — see the reader. %>
    <% progress = pool.calculator.progress_percentage %>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <div class="h-2.5 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
        <div class="h-2.5 rounded-full
             <%= if progress >= 100
                   'bg-status-success'
                 elsif progress < 0
                   'bg-status-danger'
                 else
                   'bg-brand'
                 end %>"
             style="width: <%= progress %>%;"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-between text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">
        <span>
          <% if progress.negative? %>
            <span class="text-status-danger font-medium"><%= progress %>% complete</span>
          <% else %>
            <%= progress %>% complete
          <% end %>
        </span>
        <span>Target: <%= number_to_currency(pool.target_amount) %></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# WHAT THIS POOL IS, SAID ONCE PER KIND. The account sentence is the app's existing register
      for the shape — `entries/_impact`'s honest card and `budget_page/_suggestion_rate` both say
      the money "comes out of your buffer" — and the envelope sentence is the budget block's,
      because the two blocks are on one page and must not describe one envelope two ways.

      THE SAVINGS ARM ASKS WHICH WAY THE MONEY GOES, and it is the CATEGORY that answers (2d
      whole-plan review, fix 2). It used to say "This category contributes to a shared savings
      pool" for every category handed to it, which is the opposite of the truth for an EXPENSE
      category pointing at a goal — the demo's Health → Emergency Fund and Education → Retirement
      Supplement, an ordinary shape rather than a corner. That category WITHDRAWS: under
      `PoolBalanceLedger::ENTRY_POOL_ID` its entries are subtracted from the pool's balance, and
      `EntryImpactPresenter#direction` signs them -1 on the entry form an inch from where the money
      is typed in. The pool's TYPE picks the noun; the CATEGORY's type picks the verb, exactly as
      `#contribution?` and `#goal?` split the same two questions there. %>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
    <% if pool.pool_type_savings? %>
      <% if presenter.category.savings? %>
        This category contributes to a shared goal.
      <% else %>
        This category's spending draws from a shared goal.
      <% end %>
      Other categories may contribute to it or draw from it too.
    <% elsif pool.pool_type_account? %>
      This category points at an account, and an account is your buffer. Nothing reserves money for this category, and no rule can fill an account.
    <% else %>
      This category's spending comes out of this envelope. What is in it is what there is to spend, and the rules on the Budget page are what fill it.
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <div class="flex gap-3">
    <%= link_to pool_path(pool), class: "flex-1 inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded bg-brand text-white text-sm font-medium hover:bg-brand-dark shadow-sm transition" do %>
      <%= heroicon "arrow-right", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
      <%# THE BUTTON NAMES WHAT IT OPENS. Two of the three are `Pool#noun` capitalised and the
          third deliberately is not: an account's noun is "buffer" — its ROLE in the money, which
          is what the card's heading and its balance line are about — while this button opens
          `pools/show` for the account RECORD, a page whose own subtitle reads "Bank account". A
          "View Buffer" button landing on a page headed Bank account would be the one place the
          vocabulary paid for itself in confusion. %>
      <% if pool.pool_type_account? %>
        <span>View Account</span>
      <% else %>
        <span>View <%= pool.noun.capitalize %></span>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <%= link_to edit_pool_path(pool), class: "inline-flex items-center justify-center px-3 py-2 rounded border border-gray-300 bg-white text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-50 shadow-sm transition" do %>
      <%= heroicon "pencil-square", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
